ef 17592186519316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519496 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186519321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519496 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519496 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519496 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186518991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519496 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d314da54-c3a8-55ac-8066-a2906576f626" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519528 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519528 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95091" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519528 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519528 #edb/ref 1071 "A truck travels an average distance of $21\\,\\text{mi}$ with a gallon of gas. However, $15%$ of the time, a truck travels more than $26\\,\\text{mi}$ on a gallon of gas. Assuming that the distance the truck can travel with a gallon of gas is normally distributed, find, as a percent, the proportion of journeys that the truck travels less than $15\\,\\text{mi}$ on a gallon of gas. Round your answer to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0918\\,$ | $\\,0.0901\\,$ | $\\,0.0885\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1093\\,$ | $\\,0.1075\\,$ | $\\,0.1056\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.1292\\,$ | $\\,0.1271\\,$ | $\\,0.1251\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519528 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "217980d1-12ff-5532-90b2-461f43899f04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519528 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the distance traveled by the truck with a gallon of gas. Then we have $X∼N(21,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X\\le 15).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>26)=0.15.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 26) & =1-P(X>26) \\\\  & =1-0.15 \\\\  & =0.85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 26) & =0.85 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{26-21}{\\sigma }) & =0.85 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{5}{\\sigma }) & =0.85 \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.85)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.85)≈1.0364.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sigma } & =1.0364 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.2073 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.2073} \\\\  & ≈4.8239.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X\\le 15)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 15) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{15-21}{4.8239}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.24) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.24) \\\\  & =0.1075 \\\\  & ≈10.8%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $10.8%$ of the time, a gallon of gas travels only $15\\,\\text{mi}$ or less." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519585 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519585 #edb/ref 1069 "q-133236" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519585 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519585 #edb/ref 1071 "The average height of a player in a particular professional basketball league is $78$ inches. Additionally, $15%$ of players are over $82$ inches tall. Assuming that a player's height is normally distributed, find the percentage of players over $75$ inches tall, rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1660\\,$ | $\\,0.1635\\,$ | $\\,0.1611\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.1922\\,$ | $\\,0.1894\\,$ | $\\,0.1867\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2206\\,$ | $\\,0.2177\\,$ | $\\,0.2148\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519585 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d34b6be0-737c-5c5c-907f-c713ce8e6b32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519585 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the height of a randomly selected player. Then we have $X∼N(78,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>75).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>82)=0.15.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 82) & =1-P(X>82) \\\\  & =1-0.15 \\\\  & =0.85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 82) & =0.85 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{82-78}{\\sigma }) & =0.85 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{4}{\\sigma }) & =0.85 \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.85)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.85)≈1.0364.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sigma } & =1.0364 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.2591 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.2591} \\\\  & ≈3.85951.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X>75)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>75) & =1-P(X\\le 75) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le \\frac{75-78}{3.85951}) \\\\  & ≈1-P(Z\\le -0.78) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.78) \\\\  & =1-0.2177 \\\\  & =0.7823 \\\\  & ≈78.2%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $78.2%$ of players are over $75$ inch tall." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519587 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519587 #edb/ref 1069 "q-324738" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519587 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519587 #edb/ref 1071 "The lifespan of LED light bulbs is normally distributed with a mean of $40,000$ hours and an unknown standard deviation $\\sigma .$ Tests show that $10%$ of bulbs fail before $36,000$ hours. Assuming a normal model, find the standard deviation, **rounded to the nearest integer.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $\\,z\\,$ | $0.75$ | $0.8$ | $0.85$ | $0.9$ | $0.95$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ |\n\n$\\sigma ≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$h$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519587 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d6b501ec-18d5-51e8-9a88-2c1c73e44bb9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519587 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $X$ represent the lifespan of an LED bulb. Then we have $X∼N(40,000,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<36,000)=0.10.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<36,000) & =0.10 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{36,000-40,000}{\\sigma }) & =0.10 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{4,000}{\\sigma }) & =0.10 \\\\ -\\frac{4,000}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.10).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.9)=1.2816,\n$$\n\nand therefore, by symmetry, we have\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.10)=-1.2816.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4,000}{\\sigma } & =-1.2816 \\\\ \\frac{4,000}{\\sigma } & 